---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ConfidenceTag from "../components/ConfidenceTag.astro";
import { SITE, href } from "../lib/site";
import { getAllPhotos, getDataset } from "../lib/data";

const names = getDataset().munitionById;
---

<Base
  title="Methodology"
  description="Where the numbers come from, how the headline is chosen, and what the rebuild model assumes."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="label">Methodology</p>
    <h1>How the numbers work</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      U.S. munitions inventories are classified. Nobody outside the Pentagon knows the exact count, and this site
      doesn't pretend to. What it does is collect the best public estimates and reports, keep each one tied to its
      source, and show where they agree and where they don't.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources and claims</h2>
    <p>
      Every number starts as a <strong>claim</strong> inside a <strong>report</strong>: a CSIS analysis, a news story,
      an inspector general summary. A claim records the missile, what's being measured (prewar stock, number used,
      number left, share used), the date the number describes, how the source hedged it ("about", "more than",
      "nearly"), and the exact sentence it came from.
    </p>
    <p>Each claim gets one of three tags:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <ConfidenceTag confidence="official" /> On the record from a government body: DoD, an inspector general, CRS,
        CBO.
      </li>
      <li>
        <ConfidenceTag confidence="reported" /> Journalists citing officials, documents or unnamed sources. Often the
        freshest information, but usually without a stated baseline.
      </li>
      <li>
        <ConfidenceTag confidence="estimate" /> An analyst's model. Most stock counts here come from CSIS (Mark Cancian
        and Chris Park), who rebuild inventories from DoD budget exhibits: missiles bought (P-5) and delivered (P-21),
        less 5% for testing and training, less earlier wars and transfers to Ukraine.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Quotes are checked by machine</h2>
    <p>
      Before a report is merged, a script downloads the source page and confirms every quote appears in it word for
      word. Before comparing, it lowercases both texts, treats curly and straight quotes as the same, treats every kind
      of dash (hyphen, en dash, em dash, minus) as the same, reads "…" as "...", and collapses runs of whitespace.
      Nothing else is forgiven: a quote that only paraphrases the source fails.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a source page can't be fetched, the check fails rather than passing quietly. On a pull request, the sources
      that pull request adds or changes must be reachable; older sources that happen to be down are reported but don't
      block it. The daily watch lists any older source whose page changed or disappeared in its summary.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each source is also saved to the Internet Archive's Wayback Machine, and the snapshot is linked as "Archived copy"
      next to the original. When the original page is down or refuses the check (some sites block cloud servers), the
      check reads the archived copy instead, so a quote stays verifiable even if the page is later edited or removed.
    </p>
    <p>
      PDFs are read the same way. CSIS's "Last Rounds?" report, which supplies most production figures, is a PDF; its
      quotes are rows from its tables, checked against the text extracted from the file. Sources that can't be fetched
      automatically, such as a paywalled story, are marked for manual checking.
    </p>

    <h2>How the headline is picked</h2>
    <p>
      For each missile, the big number is the newest stock-left figure we can state <em>without mixing sources</em>:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>A direct "this many left" claim, or</li>
      <li>
        Prewar stock minus the number used, when the same document gives both, plus the missiles delivered since the war
        began on February 28 where CSIS counts them. CSIS's "inventory today" bars include this year's deliveries; we
        take the gap between that bar and its own prewar-minus-used figure (39 for Patriot) and spread it evenly over
        the days since the war began. Missiles without such a reading get nothing added: the five-year procurement
        average ran three times too high for Patriot, because deliveries lag orders and many go abroad.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Ties on date go to the more specific figure: a range beats "about", which beats "at most". The share left is
      measured against the same document's prewar figure when it has one, otherwise the most recent earlier one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some newer reporting can't be converted. CNN's August report that "nearly 80%" of THAAD interceptors had been used
      doesn't say 80% <em>of what</em>, and Pentagon and CSIS baselines differ. Rather than multiplying it against
      someone else's number, the site flags it as <strong>Newer reporting</strong> next to the headline.
    </p>

    <h2>The rebuild model</h2>
    <p>
      Each missile page projects the stockpile day by day from the headline estimate, using sourced inputs listed on
      that page:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Existing orders</strong> arrive at the recent U.S. delivery rate, CSIS's five-year average of
        procurement.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The FY2027 rebuild order</strong> starts arriving on the date in DoD's delivery schedules. It's
        delivered over one production lot, but never faster than announced production capacity.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A renewed war</strong> is off by default. Turned on, it fires at the opening campaign's average rate
        (the April ceasefire tally divided by 39 days) unless you change it. Stock can't go below zero; the unmet part
        is reported as a shortfall.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>What it leaves out, on purpose:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Firing since the estimate date.</strong> CSIS's November update counts firing through November 3,
        including the lower-intensity fighting since July 8. Nothing later has been published. It's 0 by default; the
        "Firing since the estimate" slider on each missile page lets you set a monthly rate and see how much it moves
        the rebuild. Projections that start from old estimates are optimistic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Allied demand.</strong> Announced capacities cover all customers. About half of current Patriot output
        goes abroad.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Later budgets.</strong> Only the FY2027 order is modeled. FY2028 and beyond would speed things up.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The model is deliberately simple, so every input is visible and adjustable. Its results land near CSIS's own
      return-to-prewar dates for Tomahawk, THAAD and Patriot. For JASSM it is slower than CSIS, which expects large
      deliveries from recent orders.
    </p>

    <h2>How updates happen</h2>
    <p>
      A scheduled job checks a fixed list of sources (CSIS analysts' pages and news searches for each missile) once a
      day. When something new mentions stockpile numbers, Claude drafts the claims (and any model inputs the article
      states outright, like a new production rate), a script checks every quote against the page, and the draft opens as
      a pull request. A person reviews it before anything goes live. Nothing in the stockpile figures is published
      straight from an AI.
    </p>

    <h2 id="live">The live intercept log</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={href("/live/")}>live log</a> is the one exception, and it's kept apart on purpose. Every six hours a
      job reads a short list of public Telegram channels (Ukraine's Air Force, plus an OSINT aggregator for the Gulf and
      Israel) and news headlines about intercepts. Claude Haiku logs each reported count of drones or missiles launched
      and shot down, with the exact words it came from; a script throws out any count whose quote isn't in the post word
      for word. It's then published without a human review, so it stays current.
    </p>
    <p>
      That makes it fast and rough. Each entry says whether it comes from an <strong>official channel</strong> (the
      force doing the intercepting), <strong>multiple sources</strong>, or a <strong>single source</strong>. When
      sources give different counts for the same day, the log shows the range, and charts use the lower figure. The log
      never feeds the stockpile headlines or the rebuild model.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reapers">Reaper and jet losses</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={href("/reapers/")}>Reaper page</a> counts U.S. MQ-9 and MQ-1 drones lost in the war two ways, and
      never mixes them. The <strong>U.S. count</strong> comes from fleet sizes and loss figures that officials have
      given, directly or to the press, each quoted and checked like the missile data. The Air Force hasn't published its
      own tally, so this is a floor. The <strong>claimed count</strong> starts from the Revolutionary Guards' last
      stated running total and adds each new Iranian or Houthi claim the live job logs. Those claims are one-sided: they
      can double-count, and they can include drones the U.S. never flew. The page marks each claim that came with video
      and each one U.S. officials later confirmed. The job finds them in news searches and in the Middle East Spectator,
      Fotros Resistance and Tabz Live News Telegram channels, which relay Iranian and Houthi claims quickly. Those
      channels are read for shoot-down claims only, never for intercept counts.
    </p>
    <p>
      "Left, if every claim is true" is the prewar fleet less the claimed total. It's an upper bound on losses, not an
      estimate. The Army doesn't publish its Gray Eagle fleet, so the MQ-1 has losses only.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fighter and attack jets get no fleet math, since a handful of losses doesn't move a fleet of hundreds. The page
      lists each loss U.S. officials have acknowledged, split into jets shot down by the enemy and jets downed by an
      ally's fire, and shows separately how many jets Iran and the Houthis claim. A jet that was hit but landed is
      listed and not counted. The Congressional Research Service's tally counts jets lost or damaged by any cause,
      including on the ground, so it is shown on its own and never added to the rest. The same report's full list, 81
      aircraft of every kind lost or damaged by any cause, heads the page as the broadest measure of war damage.
    </p>

    <h2 id="map">The map</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={href("/map/")}>map</a> is drawn when the site builds, from the same data as the Live and Reaper
      pages. Each report is placed by the place it names, matched against a short list of countries, cities, bases and
      seas. Intercept reports rarely say where over a country, so they sit on the country that reported them. Anything
      without a place we can match is listed under the map instead of guessed. Ukraine is left off. Borders are Natural
      Earth's, via the world-atlas package.
    </p>

    <h2>Photos</h2>
    <p>
      Every photo is an official U.S. military image from{" "}
      <a href="https://www.dvidshub.net/" rel="noopener">
        DVIDS
      </a>
      , in the public domain. They show the missile, not necessarily a launch from this war.
    </p>
    <ul class="photos">
      {getAllPhotos().map(([id, p]) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{names.get(id)!.name}</strong>: {p.credit}, {p.taken.slice(0, 4)} (
          <a href={p.sourceUrl} rel="noopener">
            DVIDS {p.dvidsId}
          </a>
          )
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2 id="data">Download the data</h2>
    <p>The same data the site is built from, regenerated on every deploy:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={href("/data/claims.csv")}>Every sourced claim (CSV)</a> or <a href={href("/data/claims.json")}>JSON</a>
        : one row per figure, with its source, date, confidence and verbatim quote.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={href("/data/headlines.json")}>Current headline figures (JSON)</a>: what each missile's page leads with.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={href("/data/live-events.json")}>The live intercept log (JSON)</a>: unverified, kept apart from
        everything above.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={href("/feed.xml")}>RSS feed</a> of new sources as they're added.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Spotted a wrong number, a newer source, or a quote out of context?{" "}
      <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues/new`}>Open an issue</a>. All data lives in plain JSON in the{" "}
      <a href={SITE.repo}>repository</a>, one file per source.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={href("/")}>← Back to the tracker</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 70ch;
    margin: 40px 0 0;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(32px, 5vw, 44px);
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin: 8px 0 12px;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 18px;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 21px;
    margin: 36px 0 8px;
  }
  li {
    margin: 6px 0;
  }
</style>
